      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We are asked to compute $\\cos 150^\\circ$. To do this, we can use the angle subtraction identity for cosine, which states that $\\cos(A - B) = \\cos A \\cos B + \\sin A \\sin B$. In this case, we can express $150^\\circ$ as $180^\\circ - 30^\\circ$. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos(180^\\circ - 30^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nUsing the angle subtraction identity with $A = 180^\\circ$ and $B = 30^\\circ$, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(180^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = \\cos 180^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ + \\sin 180^\\circ \\sin 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know the values of the trigonometric functions at these angles:\n\\[\n\\cos 180^\\circ = -1, \\quad \\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\quad \\sin 180^\\circ = 0, \\quad \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = (-1) \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + 0 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the cosine of $150^\\circ$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
