      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We are given the task to compute $\\cos 150^\\circ$. One basic identity involving cosine and reference angles is useful. Specifically, the cosine function has properties about an angle and its reference angle, which allows us to find the value of the cosine function for an angle despite it being in degrees instead of radians.\n\nIn this problem, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTo find this, we start with the angle's position on the unit circle. The angle $150^\\circ$ is located in the second quadrant, where the cosine value is negative. \n\nThe reference angle for $150^\\circ$ is:\n\\[\n180^\\circ - 150^\\circ = 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThe cosine of the reference angle is:\n\\[\n\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSince $\\cos 150^\\circ$ is negative in the second quadrant:\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\cos 30^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
